WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.100
Your cells make a protein called p53 all day long.

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.540
Half of any new batch is destroyed within about 20 minutes.

3
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.660
So why build a guardian, only to destroy it?

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.000
Because a cell in danger cannot wait for a gene to be read and a protein to be

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.820
built. If the guardian is already there, and something holds it back, the cell only

6
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:23.920
has to let go.

7
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:30.367
p53 is a transcription factor, a protein that binds DNA and switches genes on.

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:34.067
Its restraint is a protein called MDM2.

9
00:00:35.087 --> 00:00:38.447
MDM2 grips a short piece at the front end of p53.

10
00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:41.567
That grip covers the part that switches genes on.

11
00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:46.867
It also tags p53 with ubiquitin, a mark that sends it to the proteasome to be

12
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:47.867
destroyed.

13
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.960
Now DNA breaks across both strands.

14
00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.960
That switches on a kinase called ATM.

15
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.400
ATM and the kinases beneath it add phosphates to MDM2.

16
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.360
They also add phosphates to the stretch of p53 that MDM2 grips.

17
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.660
The grip loosens, and destruction slows sharply.

18
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.180
So p53 piles up without any new gene being read.

19
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.820
Free of its restraint, it switches on the gene for p21.

20
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.500
That protein blocks the kinases that drive cell division.

21
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.700
It switches on genes for DNA repair as well.

22
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.040
If the damage is severe, it also switches on genes that kill the cell.

23
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.610
Chemical threats use the same design, with a responder called Nrf2.

24
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.090
Nrf2 switches on genes for protective enzymes, and the cell makes it constantly.

25
00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.950
Keap1 grips it as part of a ubiquitin ligase that tags it for destruction.

26
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.730
Then a reactive chemical hits Keap1.

27
00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.230
On the current view, tagging stops, so new Nrf2 goes to the nucleus.

28
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.610
Put the two side by side.

29
00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.490
In each, a responder is made all the time.

30
00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.030
Something holds it back, or destroys it.

31
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.030
Stress removes the restraint, and the responder is already there.

32
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.690
So keep this rule: a stress responder is made all the time and held back, until

33
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.290
stress removes the restraint.
